# Prove session destruction and temporary cleanup

## Scope and existing evidence

Close, refresh, cancel, crash, and restart fixtures must show that ephemeral
state and media intermediates disappear while explicit exports remain intact.

Current runtime evidence proves orderly process termination releases the old
loopback listener, restart generates a fresh session credential, and a stale
credential cannot authenticate to the new process. A colocated runtime fixture
now drops its owner and immediately rebinds the exact OS-assigned loopback
endpoint, pinning listener release directly.

The secret adapter separately pins that independent operating-system-backed
generations do not reuse the same encoded credential. Process-level draft
fixtures
also write and read task, source, and raw-response text, then verify both
orderly restart and forced process death yield empty fields in the fresh
session. A checked-in application-process fixture now also populates an accepted
semantic revision containing two semantic asset records and a figure reference,
retains distinct raw byte sequences for both assets, commits text and
asset-reference history, and leaves one terminal media job with a registered
waveform plus retry-required cleanup bookkeeping in process memory. Orderly exit
and forced process
death both leave a fresh owner with no accepted revision, history position, raw
bytes, or prior media-job authority.

A 128-job interleaving regression additionally verifies that independent
process-local media jobs preserve their own cleanup requirements, failed
cleanup retries, and settled states even when supplied with another job's
waveform identity. This is in-memory bookkeeping only, not actual file cleanup.

Media decoding and actual temporary
conversion-file cleanup remain open because no filesystem intermediate exists
yet.

While private draft text is live, the runtime changes no declared repository
runtime-root file. The fuller application-process fixture also proves on Linux
that populated draft, semantic history, retained asset bytes, derived results,
and retry-required media cleanup add no writable regular-file descriptors beyond
process descriptors inherited from the test harness.

Checked-in browser policy tests guard one-time launch-credential fragment
consumption, credential invalidation on `pagehide`, in-flight handshake and
draft request cancellation, pending clipboard/draft invalidation, session-text
clearing, bfcache subtree scrubbing, and mandatory reload when a persisted page
is restored. A `401`
during handshake, draft
hydration, or draft replacement now invalidates the same page-session work and
scrubs all four session text surfaces before requiring restart.

Aborted draft requests and stale `413` diagnostic-body completions cannot
repopulate status text after invalidation. Concurrent field failures also remain
visible until the failed field succeeds; another field cannot announce
`Session ready` while work or failure remains. A real Firefox coalescing fixture
now holds one task replacement, edits the field again, returns a valid `413` for
the obsolete value, and requires the newer complete value to be posted next and
restore `Session ready`.

A separate real Firefox fixture hydrates all three draft fields through a
deterministic same-origin session,
confirms the launch fragment has been scrubbed, reloads that exact tab, and
requires the refreshed document to expose blank disabled draft fields without
issuing another handshake or draft request. Refresh therefore cannot recover the
launch credential from the rewritten browser URL or persistence API.

A second real Firefox path hydrates the page, holds an authenticated task
replacement in flight, closes that exact browsing context, and observes the
request transport abort while Firefox remains alive. This covers browser-close
cancellation of page-session draft work, not cancellation of backend application
operations.

Handshake response admission now invalidates `401` directly from the status and
parses JSON bodies only for the two body-bearing handshake result classes,
`200` and `409`; unrelated statuses cannot delay failure on an irrelevant body.
The localhost adapter also JSON-escapes compatible and required backend version
identities, so application-owned quotes, backslashes, controls, and Unicode
cannot corrupt the handshake response syntax.

An explicit, confirmation-gated **End session** control now sends an
exact-origin Bearer-authenticated, empty-body POST while the page remains
active. The backend rejects missing or incorrect origin/credential and
nonempty bodies; on acceptance it acknowledges closure, leaves its loopback
accept loop, releases the listener, and drops the process application owner.

A real socket regression verifies that an unauthorized attempt cannot end the
session and that an authorized request releases the exact endpoint. Firefox
coverage verifies confirmation before any mutation, authenticated termination,
and immediate local draft erasure. Authenticated browser POST requests use an
origin-only referrer policy so Firefox supplies the canonical Origin header
without revealing a URL path or fragment.

GET hydration still suppresses the referrer. Browser `pagehide` continues
to wipe local state independently: network teardown is not promised for tabs
that close without the explicit command, or for crashed or unresponsive
processes.

Browser draft hydration now admits an HTTP 200 body only when its response
declares the expected `text/plain; charset=utf-8` media type. A red-first
runtime-extracted browser regression showed a 200 HTML response previously
became accepted draft text. The corrected path refuses wrong or absent media
without reading the response body.

Hydration leaves all three inputs disabled
until every field passes and can never apply a partial snapshot. The independent
browser transport fixture also pins case-insensitive exact media admission.

A real Firefox loopback fixture now returns an HTML media type with a
successful status for the source draft after the task draft has arrived. It
verifies the complete three-field snapshot stays blank and disabled, the
candidate field is not requested, and a later valid-media retry hydrates all
fields. This covers browser network and DOM behavior beyond the isolated parser
fixture.

The end confirmation can be cancelled by Escape or by moving focus away from
its button, preventing a stale armed action. An authenticated end returning
`401` now invalidates browser authority and clears private draft fields. A
transient `503` retains the draft and resets confirmation so the user can
retry without falsely announcing successful destruction. The Firefox suite
asserts both outcomes and a successful subsequent retry, in addition to
confirmation cancellation and cleanup.

A Linux process fixture now completes an explicitly selected profile Export,
forcibly kills its process after the synced file effect, and verifies that the
exported ZIP remains readable through the independent secure selected-profile
Import path in a fresh process owner. The ZIP includes a syntax-valid JSON
section and opaque binary asset, both independently SHA-verified after process
death.

The new owner has no inherited Export
retry receipt and cannot replay the same key over a preserve-existing target;
no Export staging file remains after the successful commit. The readiness
marker is test-only and removed before checking surviving directory entries.

A separate low-level Linux file-commit fixture now kills a process while
writing an incomplete profile through anonymous `O_TMPFILE` staging. Both
preserve-existing and replacement dispositions leave no named partial stage;
the original replacement target survives unchanged. This is not a proof of
cleanup if the process dies in the brief replacement link-before-rename window.
The test-owned readiness marker is removed after each forced kill.

The same Linux child-process harness now also suspends the final cancellation
observation **after** the full anonymous file has been synced but **before**
its first link, then kills the child with SIGKILL. Both preserve-existing and
replacement runs prove no staged filename escapes and the replacement target
remains unchanged. The harness verifies actual SIGKILL termination, not only
an unsuccessful exit. This extends the precommit crash proof to synced bytes;
it still does not cover a crash in the later link-before-rename window.

The task remains open until temporary media exist and end-to-end fixtures also
cover backend operation cancellation, media cleanup, and late-link crash
recovery rather than only anonymous-stage and post-sync termination.
